WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
What's up, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.100
Episode 73, it's June 16th, 2026.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.120
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.280
Let's dive in.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.360
Something while it just happened with a new AI model

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.560
that could change how you work on school projects

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.680
or creative ideas.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.880
A researcher who tries out fresh tools every week

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.960
shared that Fable, a new model from Anthropic

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.740
feels like a real jump ahead of earlier versions.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.160
It does not just answer questions or write a bit of text.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.460
Instead, it can take on bigger, more complex tasks all at once.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.020
Think of it like your phone's auto-complete

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.700
suddenly learning how to finish entire paragraphs

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.160
while still sounding like your own writing style.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.840
Another way to picture it is upgrading from a basic calculator

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.920
that only adds numbers to one that can also explain

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.160
why the answer makes sense

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.900
and then suggests the next problem worth solving.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.320
These bigger jumps are starting to show up more often

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.660
across different companies, not just one lab.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.440
This matters for anyone using AI for a TikTok video

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.440
or a history essay because the tools are getting better

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.460
at understanding what you actually want

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.440
instead of just guessing the next word.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.480
For future careers that mix creativity with technology,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.380
learning how to guide these tools now gives you a real edge.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.800
You can try something similar right now

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.440
by opening a free chat GPT or Cloud account

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.780
on your phone or laptop.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.100
Ask it to rewrite a paragraph from your own writing

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.780
in a completely different style

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.060
like turning a serious school report

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.360
into a casual text message to a friend.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.660
Watch how it keeps your main points

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.780
while changing the tone.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.040
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.460
the deep dive where we look under the hood

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.900
at how one piece of AI actually works.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.360
Today we are exploring how AI music tools

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.780
get trained on huge collections of songs.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.620
You know how when you listen to a playlist,

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.020
Spotify notices the songs you skip

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.340
and the ones you replay,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.900
then quietly adds more tracks that match

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.260
what you have already liked.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.820
Now imagine that same system gets way better

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.840
at noticing patterns across millions of songs at once,

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.600
not just your personal list.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.000
It starts spotting tiny details

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.760
like how certain beats or lyrics appear together.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.440
Even across artists you have never heard.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.500
Next, picture the system using those patterns

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.820
to create brand new songs that sound like

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.780
they could have come from your favorite artists

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.600
because it has studied so many examples.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.960
And that is basically what happens

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.960
when an AI music model gets trained

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.480
on huge collections of songs.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.160
It does not copy any single track.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.420
Instead it learns the hidden rules

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.080
that make music feel familiar or surprising.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.620
So next time someone says training data,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.100
you can tell them it is basically the same idea

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.040
as a playlist that learned your taste.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.780
Except the playlist has millions of songs

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.440
and can now invent new ones.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.020
Not so scary, right?

